//? Title: Feature pyramids for multiscale detection
//? Subtitle: Tensor computations, explicit checks and reproducible local figures
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Objects at different scales need both spatial detail and useful high-level features. This report verifies tensor top-down/lateral fusion and scale-based region routing, then fits the full compact source FPN detector through an explicit joint Adam loop and plots actual held-out proposals, detections and learning curves.
)
\toc

Source example: `examples/cpp/source/nn/1_convolutional_neural_networks/1.6_object_detection/fpn.cpp`.

> The method

FPN projects each backbone scale to a shared width, adds resized deeper inner features, and independently smooths every output. A multi-level shared RPN proposes regions; each region chooses one pyramid level before the shared box head. The source64-pixel adaptation below preserves these mechanisms while using a small learned convolutional backbone and canonical RoI side32.

The editable concept diagram is `diagrams/fpn_concept.tikz`. Regeneration uses
LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, plus dvisvgm; exports use the saved SVG.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/fpn_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/fpn_concept.tikz > build/fpn_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/fpn_concept.svg build/fpn_concept.dvi > build/fpn_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered local concept diagram.\n'
```
