> Simple example: unsmoothed top-down fusion on odd grids

Let `I_l = lateral(C_l) + resize_nearest(I_(l+1))` and
`P_l = smooth_3x3(I_l)`. The top-down branch carries **I**, not P: propagating
smoothed maps would repeatedly filter and rescale deeper features. Every lateral
is a linear 1x1 convolution; every output has its own linear 3x3 smoothing filter.
No ReLU or batch norm clips negative responses. Resizing to the lateral's actual
height/width handles odd rectangular grids, rather than assuming exact doubling.

The controlled maps below have shapes 7x9, 4x5 and 2x3. Lateral weights are one;
smoothing filters have only a central coefficient, respectively 2, 3 and 4.
An independent nearest-index oracle checks all output values and every input
derivative of the summed outputs. All four displayed maps share one colour range.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/fpn_fusion.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <print>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  namespace m = n::models;
  const int H[] = {7, 4, 2}, W[] = {9, 5, 3};
  const auto type = n::DType::Float64;
  n::ParameterStore p(17, type);
  std::vector<n::Tensor> input;
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> values;
  for (int l = 0; l < 3; ++l) {
    const auto x = n::sin(.4 * n::arange(0, H[l] * W[l]).to(type) + l)
                       .reshape({1, H[l], W[l], 1})
                       .detach()
                       .requires_grad_();
    input.push_back(x);
    values.push_back(x.to_vector<double>());
  }
  {
    n::NoGradGuard guard;
    m::FeaturePyramid(p, "example", input, 1);
    for (int l = 0; l < 3; ++l) {
      const auto prefix = "example/level" + std::to_string(l + 2);
      p.set(prefix + "/lateral/W", n::ones({1, 1, 1, 1}, type));
      p.set(prefix + "/smooth/W",
            n::one_hot(n::from_vector(std::vector<std::int64_t>{4}), 9, 1, 0, type)
                    .reshape({3, 3, 1, 1}) *
                double(l + 2));
    }
  }
  const auto f = m::FeaturePyramid(p, "example", input, 1);
  n::Tensor loss = n::zeros({}, type);
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> expected;
  for (int l = 0; l < 3; ++l) {
    loss = loss + n::sum(f.levels[l]);
    expected.emplace_back(H[l] * W[l], 0.);
  }
  double value_error = 0, gradient_error = 0;
  int negative = 0;
  // Independent host oracle: this checks the tensor model rather than implementing it.
  for (int l = 0; l < 3; ++l)
    for (int r = 0; r < H[l]; ++r)
      for (int c = 0; c < W[l]; ++c) {
        int rr = r, cc = c;
        double sum = 0;
        for (int j = l; j < 3; ++j) {
          if (j > l) {
            rr = rr * H[j] / H[j - 1];
            cc = cc * W[j] / W[j - 1];
          }
          sum += values[j][rr * W[j] + cc];
          expected[j][rr * W[j] + cc] += l + 2;
        }
        value_error =
            std::max(value_error, std::abs(f.levels[l].at({0, r, c, 0}) - (l + 2) * sum));
        negative += f.levels[l].at({0, r, c, 0}) < 0;
      }
  const auto gradients = n::grad(loss, input);
  for (int l = 0; l < 3; ++l)
    for (int i = 0; i < H[l] * W[l]; ++i)
      gradient_error =
          std::max(gradient_error,
                   std::abs(gradients[l].reshape({H[l] * W[l]}).at({i}) - expected[l][i]));
  if (value_error > 1e-12 || gradient_error > 1e-12 || !negative ||
      f.levels[3].shape() != n::Shape{1, 1, 2, 1})
    return 1;
  if (f.levels[3].at({0, 0, 1, 0}) != f.levels[2].at({0, 0, 2, 0}))
    return 1;
  const double limit = n::max(n::abs(f.levels[0])).item() + .1;
  const auto map = [&](const n::Tensor &x, const std::string &title) {
    return n::plot_tensor_map(n::reverse(x.reshape({7, 9}), {0}), -limit, limit)
        .title(title)
        .x_label("column")
        .y_label("row")
        .x_limits(-.5, 8.5)
        .y_limits(-.5, 6.5);
  };
  const auto up = n::resize_nearest(f.inner[1], {7, 9});
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(2, 2)
      .add(map(input[0], "C2 input"))
      .add(map(up, "Upsampled C3 inner"))
      .add(map(f.inner[0], "Unsmoothed inner P2"))
      .add(map(f.levels[0], "Smoothed output P2"))
      .size(1200, 1000)
      .fit_panels()
      .save_svg("build/fpn_fusion.svg");
  std::print("Odd-grid value error {:.12f}; every-input gradient error {:.12f}; {} negative "
             "output values preserved. Extra coarser output is 1x2 and samples coarsest "
             "columns 0 and 2 exactly. Shared plotted range [{:.3f},{:.3f}].\n",
             value_error, gradient_error, negative, -limit, limit);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Odd-grid value error 0.000000000000; every-input gradient error 0.000000000000; 35 negative output values preserved. Extra coarser output is 1x2 and samples coarsest columns 0 and 2 exactly. Shared plotted range [-5.754,5.754].
\image(figures/fpn_fusion.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Controlled three-level tensor fusion: C2 plus resized unsmoothed C3 forms inner P2, then a separate central-coefficient filter doubles that map. The same numerical colour range is used throughout; the independent check covers all levels and gradients.)
\alttext(Controlled three-level tensor fusion: C2 plus resized unsmoothed C3 forms inner P2, then a separate central-coefficient filter doubles that map. The same numerical colour range is used throughout; the independent check covers all levels and gradients.)

The optional coarser map uses a 1x1 stride-two VALID max pool, so it selects
positions 0, 2, and so on without another filter or activation. In the full
four-input C2–C5 detector this map is P6; the small three-input fixture above
simply tests an extra coarser output.

> Simple example: route each region to its scale

The paper's rule assigns pixel box side `s=sqrt(max(height*width,1e-6))` to
`clamp(floor(4+log2(s/canonical)),2,5)`. The default canonical side is 224;
this 64-pixel detection adaptation uses 32. Routing is discrete. Each box is
pooled only on its assigned level, and a tensor gather restores original order.
The pooling operation is the source's 14x14 bilinear crop plus 2x2 max pool,
not ROIAlign. Box coordinates are constants for feature sampling.

Constant feature maps equal to their level IDs make routing directly observable.
Both extremes are clamped in this fixture, and the selected pooled value should
be exactly the chosen level everywhere.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/fpn_routing.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <print>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  namespace m = n::models;
  n::NoGradGuard guard;
  std::vector<m::DetectionPyramidLevel> maps;
  for (int k = 2; k <= 5; ++k) {
    const int cells = 2048 / (1 << k);
    maps.push_back({n::full({1, cells, cells, 1}, double(k)), 1 << k});
  }
  const auto sides = n::tensor({32., 64., 112., 224., 448., 896.}), start = n::ones({6}) * 64.;
  const auto boxes = n::stack({start, start, start + sides, start + sides}, 1);
  const auto indices = n::zeros({6}, n::DType::Int64);
  const auto levels = m::PyramidDetectionLevels(boxes, 224),
             pool = m::PyramidDetectionRoiPool(maps, boxes, indices, 224);
  if (levels.to_vector<int>() != std::vector<int>{2, 2, 3, 4, 5, 5})
    return 1;
  for (int i = 0; i < 6; ++i)
    if (n::max(n::abs(n::slice_axis(pool, 0, i, i + 1) - levels.at({i}))).item() > 1e-6)
      return 1;
  auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.points(sides.to_vector<double>(), levels.to_vector<double>(), "selected level",
           {37, 99, 235}, 5.)
      .title("Pyramid region assignment")
      .x_label("pixel box side")
      .y_label("assigned P level")
      .x_limits(0, 950)
      .y_limits(1.5, 6.2);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  p.size(900, 500).save_svg("build/fpn_routing.svg");
  std::print("| side | assigned level | pooled value |\n|---:|---:|---:|\n");
  for (int i = 0; i < 6; ++i)
    std::print("| {:.0f} | {:.0f} | {:.6f} |\n", sides.at({i}), levels.at({i}),
               pool.at({i, 0, 0, 0}));
}
```
